Article 21 Where a network data processor, prior to processing personal information, informs the individual according to law by formulating personal information processing rules, such rules shall be displayed in a centralized manner, be easily accessible and placed in a prominent position, with content that is specific, clear and easy to understand, including but not limited to the following:
(1) the name or title and contact information of the network data processor;
(2) the purpose, method and type of processing of personal information, the necessity of processing sensitive personal information and the impact on the individual's rights and interests;
(3) the retention period of personal information and the method of processing upon expiry; where the retention period is difficult to determine, the method for determining the retention period shall be specified;
(4) the methods and channels for individuals to access, copy, transfer, correct, supplement, delete, restrict the processing of their personal information, and to cancel accounts or withdraw consent.
Where a network data processor, in accordance with the preceding paragraph, informs the individual of the purpose, method and type of collection of personal information and provision of personal information to other network data processors, as well as the information of the recipient of the network data, it shall list such matters in the form of a list or the like. Where a network data processor processes personal information of a minor under the age of 14, it shall also formulate special personal information processing rules.

Article 22 Where a network data processor processes personal information based on an individual's consent, it shall comply with the following provisions:
(1) collection of personal information shall be necessary for providing products or services; the network data processor shall not collect personal information beyond the necessary scope, and shall not obtain the individual's consent through misleading, fraudulent, coercive or other improper means;
(2) where processing sensitive personal information such as biometric data, religious belief, specific identity, medical health, financial accounts, or whereabouts, the individual's separate consent shall be obtained;
(3) where processing personal information of a minor under the age of 14, the consent of the minor's parents or other guardians shall be obtained;
(4) the network data processor shall not process personal information beyond the purpose, method, type and retention period consented to by the individual;
(5) the network data processor shall not repeatedly seek consent after the individual has explicitly expressed objection to the processing of his or her personal information;
(6) where the purpose, method or type of processing of personal information changes, the individual's consent shall be obtained anew.
Where laws or administrative regulations provide that written consent shall be obtained for processing sensitive personal information, such provisions shall prevail.

Article 25 For a request to transfer personal information that meets the following conditions, the network data processor shall provide a channel for another network data processor designated by the individual to access and obtain such personal information:
(1) the true identity of the requesting person can be verified;
(2) the requested transfer concerns personal information that the individual has consented to provide or that has been collected based on a contract;
(3) the transfer of personal information is technically feasible;
(4) the transfer of personal information does not impair the lawful rights and interests of others.
Where the number of requests for transfer or other aspects clearly exceed a reasonable scope, the network data processor may collect necessary fees based on the cost of transferring the personal information.

Article 30 A processor of important data shall designate a network data security responsible person and establish a network data security management body. The network data security management body shall fulfill the following responsibilities for network data security protection:
(1) formulating and implementing the network data security management system, operating procedures and emergency response plan for network data security incidents;
(2) periodically organizing and carrying out activities such as network data security risk monitoring, risk assessment, emergency drills, publicity, education and training, and promptly handling network data security risks and incidents;
(3) accepting and handling complaints and reports on network data security.
The network data security responsible person shall possess professional knowledge of network data security and relevant management work experience, be appointed from among the members of the management of the network data processor, and have the right to directly report network data security information to the relevant competent authority.
A network data processor that holds important data of a specific type or scale as prescribed by the relevant competent authority shall conduct a security background check on the network data security responsible person and personnel in key positions, and strengthen the training of such personnel. When conducting such checks, it may request assistance from a public security organ or a State security organ.

Article 31 Before providing, entrusting for processing, or jointly processing important data, a processor of important data shall conduct a risk assessment, except where such actions are for the performance of statutory duties or legal obligations.
The risk assessment shall focus on the following:
(1) whether the purpose, method, scope, etc. of providing, entrusting for processing, or jointly processing network data, as well as the processing of network data by the recipient, are lawful, legitimate and necessary;
(2) the risk of the provided, entrusted or jointly processed network data being tampered with, destroyed, disclosed, illegally obtained or illegally used, and the risks posed to national security, public interest or the lawful rights and interests of individuals or organizations;
(3) the integrity and law-abiding status of the recipient of the network data;
(4) whether the requirements concerning network data security in the relevant contracts concluded or to be concluded with the recipient can effectively bind the recipient to fulfill its obligation of network data security protection;
(5) whether the technical and management measures taken or to be taken can effectively prevent risks such as the network data being tampered with, destroyed, disclosed, illegally obtained or illegally used;
(6) other assessment contents prescribed by the relevant competent authority.

Article 33 A processor of important data shall conduct a risk assessment on its network data processing activities annually and submit a risk assessment report to the relevant competent authority at or above the provincial level; the relevant competent authority shall promptly inform the cyberspace administration and the public security organ at the same level.
The risk assessment report shall include the following:
(1) basic information of the network data processor, information on the network data security management body, the name and contact information of the network data security responsible person, etc.;
(2) the purpose, type, quantity, method, scope, storage period, storage location, etc. of processing important data, and the situation of conducting network data processing activities, excluding the content of the network data itself;
(3) the network data security management system and its implementation, technical measures such as encryption, backup, labeling, access control, security authentication, and other necessary measures, and their effectiveness;
(4) identified network data security risks, network data security incidents that have occurred and their handling;
(5) the risk assessment status of providing, entrusting for processing, or jointly processing important data;
(6) the cross-border transfer of network data;
(7) other reporting contents prescribed by the relevant competent authority.
Where a large network platform service provider that processes important data submits a risk assessment report, in addition to the contents specified in the preceding paragraph, it shall also fully explain the network data security situation of its key business and supply chain.
Where a processor of important data has important data processing activities that may jeopardize national security, the relevant competent authority at or above the provincial level shall order it to take measures such as rectification or cessation of processing important data. The processor of important data shall immediately take measures in accordance with the relevant requirements.

Article 35 A network data processor may provide personal information abroad if it meets any of the following conditions:
(1) having passed the data cross-border security assessment organized by the national cyberspace administration;
(2) having undergone personal information protection certification by a professional institution in accordance with the provisions of the national cyberspace administration;
(3) complying with the provisions of the national cyberspace administration on standard contracts for the cross-border transfer of personal information;
(4) it is truly necessary to provide personal information abroad for the conclusion or performance of a contract to which the individual is a party;
(5) it is truly necessary to provide employee personal information abroad for cross-border human resources management implemented in accordance with labor rules and regulations formulated according to law and collective contracts concluded according to law;
(6) it is truly necessary to provide personal information abroad for the performance of statutory duties or legal obligations;
(7) it is truly necessary to provide personal information abroad under emergency circumstances to protect the life, health or property of a natural person;
(8) other conditions prescribed by laws, administrative regulations or the national cyberspace administration.

Article 46 A large network platform service provider shall not use network data, algorithms, platform rules, etc. to engage in the following activities:
(1) processing network data generated by users on the platform through misleading, fraudulent, coercive or other improper means;
(2) without legitimate reasons, restricting users from accessing or using the network data they generate on the platform;
(3) imposing unreasonable differential treatment on users, harming their lawful rights and interests;
(4) other activities prohibited by laws or administrative regulations.

Article 50 The relevant competent authority may take the following measures to supervise and inspect network data security:
(1) requiring the network data processor and its relevant personnel to provide explanations on matters subject to supervision and inspection;
(2) consulting and copying documents and records related to network data security;
(3) inspecting the operation of network data security measures;
(4) inspecting equipment and articles related to network data processing activities;
(5) other necessary measures prescribed by laws or administrative regulations.
A network data processor shall cooperate with the relevant competent authority in conducting network data security supervision and inspection according to law.
